From 347def5552b94a8898d1bd3620e5e618b74400dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 13:02:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Speed up Inkling 2 decode: tensor-core attention, split
 windows, fused small steps

Decode read the KV cache once per row (a verify of 8 rows read it 8
times) and at 180K spent about 40% of a token in attention, the rest
largely in small kernels and waits on the other card or the CPU.

k_attn_dreg is decode attention on the tensor cores straight from
global memory: a block's 16 rows (up to 5 tokens) read each key once;
the dot products' terms and P V's keys are reordered so every thread
reads whole 16-byte pieces of its rows into its mma fragments, int8 to
FP16 exactly (byte permutes) with the per-key scales in FP32. Keys a
block: 16 blocks a KV head, at least 256, and at most 2048 up to 64
blocks a KV head.

With two cards, each tiered full-attention window is split in 64-cell
blocks between them (every other block on the other card, written
there by peer stores): a decode step has the other card read its half
at the same time (its partials folded into ours); a short prompt read
has it read its half of the chunk (the two running results joined);
a read of 16K tokens or more goes back to whole windows, both cards
busy with its chunks, and splits again after it. Peer access is on,
so the cards' copies to each other go straight over PCIe.

The NVFP4 down rows of Inkling's n_ff (1152) get their own kernel
(native_down_nv4_kernel: 8 lanes a row, the group's activations in
shared memory, the same sums): the generic rows ran at half the
card's bandwidth. A looped layer's controls take two launches for q,
k and v together instead of fifteen; a decode's short convolutions,
their history, the residual add and the experts' weighted sum one;
k_mix spreads a row over 4 blocks.

The expert pool ranks experts by routings, not routings per byte (a
routing in RAM costs the CPU its bytes, the bytes are the VRAM it
holds): per byte emptied the Q8_0 layers first, 6.6 of 8 entries of
their visits on the CPU. A visit's CPU job writes into mapped host
memory and raises a flag; the GPU waits on it only for an entry whose
expert is in RAM, never for a visit with none.

2x RTX 3060, UD-Q4_K_XL, 180K-token prompt, 512 tokens, no drafting:
28.4 -> 35.6 tok/s; 1K: 43.6 -> 50.0; prompt 1418 tok/s either way.
Logits against the same build before (KL over 1000 positions) within
the spread two equal builds' rounding gives; each new kernel checked
against the one it replaces on the same inputs (attention 1e-4, the
down rows 1e-7).

The second prompt executor's room counts a split window's buffers
(it ran out of VRAM in its init at 2x512K), and its log line counts
the experts it moved (it counted some slabs as experts, 4x).
